        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 1425,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 629,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 702,
            "height": 1066,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "hsv_to_hsl converts a colour described as hue, saturation, value into the same colour described as hue, saturation, lightness.\n\nAn orange of [30, 1, 1] in hsv comes out as [30, 1, 0.5] in hsl. In the demo, one component of the hsv colour sweeps steadily while the other two hold still; the vectors show the colour going in and coming out, and the swatch shows the colour itself (through a second, small hsv_to_rgb node beside the input, as a swatch needs red, green and blue).\n\nBoth describe a colour by its hue; they differ in what the other two numbers mean. Use it when a colour comes as hsv (or hsb) and something wants hsl.\n\nTHE TWO SPACES:\n\nhsv   hue, saturation, value. Like hsl, but value runs black to the pure\n      colour rather than through to white. Handy when you think of it as\n      \"how much light is on this\". Design tools often call it hsb.\nhsl   hue, saturation, lightness. Hue is the colour itself as an angle\n      round the wheel, saturation is how vivid, lightness runs black to white\n      with the pure colour at the middle.\n\nSYNTAX:\nhsv_to_hsl\n\nEXAMPLE:\nhsv_to_hsl\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThree numbers - a list, or a NumPy array - in the hsv space. Receiving data here triggers the conversion.\n\nfrom / to:\nThe spaces being converted between: rgb, cmy, hsl or hsv. The node's name sets them to hsv and hsl; the menus can repoint it to any other pair without replacing the node.\n\nin scale / out scale:\nWhat numeric range the values use, INDEPENDENTLY at each end:\n\n  0-1     0.0 to 1.0, the usual choice inside a patch\n  0-100   percentages, as design tools tend to use\n  0-255   bytes, as image files and many devices use\n\nBecause the two are separate, the node also does the rescaling for you:\nset in scale to 0-255 and out scale to 0-1 to bring an image's colours into patch range in the same move as the conversion.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe converted colour: three numbers (a NumPy array) in the hsl space and the out scale.\n\nHUE IS ALWAYS IN DEGREES:\nThe scale settings apply to every component EXCEPT hue, which is always 0 to 360 whatever the scale says. Hue is an angle, not a proportion. An hsl value like [210, 0.5, 0.4] with a 0-1 scale is correct, not a bug.\n\nOUT OF RANGE:\nValues are converted through rgb, and red, green and blue are held to 0 to 1 on the way, so a component pushed past its range gives the nearest colour that exists rather than an error.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nhsl_to_hsv    the reverse conversion\nhsv_to_rgb    hsv to red, green, blue\nhsv_to_cmy    hsv to cyan, magenta, yellow\ncolor_convert    choose both spaces yourself\ncolor            pick a colour, or show one",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 694,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "hsv_to_hsl converts a colour described as hue, saturation, value into the same colour described as hue, saturation, lightness.\n\nAn orange of [30, 1, 1] in hsv comes out as [30, 1, 0.5] in hsl. In the demo, one component of the hsv colour sweeps steadily while the other two hold still; the vectors show the colour going in and coming out, and the swatch shows the colour itself (through a second, small hsv_to_rgb node beside the input, as a swatch needs red, green and blue).\n\nBoth describe a colour by its hue; they differ in what the other two numbers mean. Use it when a colour comes as hsv (or hsb) and something wants hsl.\n\nTHE TWO SPACES:\n\nhsv   hue, saturation, value. Like hsl, but value runs black to the pure\n      colour rather than through to white. Handy when you think of it as\n      \"how much light is on this\". Design tools often call it hsb.\nhsl   hue, saturation, lightness. Hue is the colour itself as an angle\n      round the wheel, saturation is how vivid, lightness runs black to white\n      with the pure colour at the middle.\n\nSYNTAX:\nhsv_to_hsl\n\nEXAMPLE:\nhsv_to_hsl\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThree numbers - a list, or a NumPy array - in the hsv space. Receiving data here triggers the conversion.\n\nfrom / to:\nThe spaces being converted between: rgb, cmy, hsl or hsv. The node's name sets them to hsv and hsl; the menus can repoint it to any other pair without replacing the node.\n\nin scale / out scale:\nWhat numeric range the values use, INDEPENDENTLY at each end:\n\n  0-1     0.0 to 1.0, the usual choice inside a patch\n  0-100   percentages, as design tools tend to use\n  0-255   bytes, as image files and many devices use\n\nBecause the two are separate, the node also does the rescaling for you:\nset in scale to 0-255 and out scale to 0-1 to bring an image's colours into patch range in the same move as the conversion.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nout:\nThe converted colour: three numbers (a NumPy array) in the hsl space and the out scale.\n\nHUE IS ALWAYS IN DEGREES:\nThe scale settings apply to every component EXCEPT hue, which is always 0 to 360 whatever the scale says. Hue is an angle, not a proportion. An hsl value like [210, 0.5, 0.4] with a 0-1 scale is correct, not a bug.\n\nOUT OF RANGE:\nValues are converted through rgb, and red, green and blue are held to 0 to 1 on the way, so a component pushed past its range gives the nearest colour that exists rather than an error.\n\nSEE ALSO:\nhsl_to_hsv    the reverse conversion\nhsv_to_rgb    hsv to red, green, blue\nhsv_to_cmy    hsv to cyan, magenta, yellow\ncolor_convert    choose both spaces yourself\ncolor            pick a colour, or show one"
